      "inputTokens": 89,
      "outputTokens": 638,
      "costUsd": 0.0057865,
      "pronunciationText": "Here are two readings from the tide board at a harbour boat ramp: high tide, seven and a half metres, at time zero, and low tide, one and a half metres, six hours later. The water keeps rising and falling in the same pattern, about twice a day. A quantity that repeats like that is called periodic, and a transformed cos curve can describe it. Let's build that curve one feature at a time."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-t03/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 854,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.8409999999999851,
        "after": "five.",
        "before": "The"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "five",
          "start": 9.323,
          "end": 9.623
        },
        {
          "text": "three",
          "start": 22.144999999999985,
          "end": 22.41166666666665
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 28.44,
      "peakFraction": 0.857086181640625,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 91,
      "outputTokens": 711,
      "costUsd": 0.006444500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Start with the middle. The water swings as far above its middle level as below it, so the midline is halfway between high and low: seven point five plus one point five, over two, is four point five. The amplitude is the distance from the midline up to high tide: seven point five minus one point five, over two, is three. So the curve runs from four point five minus three, up to four point five plus three."
    },
    {
